<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations</h1><p class="st-meta">1636 CE – 1776 CE · capital Newport</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The New England colony founded by Roger Williams on the principle of religious liberty, the first to separate church and state in law.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Banished from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/massachusetts-bay-colony">Massachusetts Bay Colony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-williams">Roger Williams</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/providence-rhode-island">Providence</a> in 1636 on land obtained from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narragansett-people">Narragansett</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anne-hutchinson">Anne Hutchinson's</a> followers founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portsmouth-rhode-island">Portsmouth</a> in 1638, a group split off to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newport-rhode-island">Newport</a> in 1639, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-gorton">Samuel Gorton</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick-rhode-island">Warwick</a> in 1642. Williams won a parliamentary patent uniting the towns in 1644.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The royal charter of 1663, obtained by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-clarke-baptist-minister">John Clarke</a>, guaranteed full liberty in religious concerns, and the colony became a home for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptists">Baptists</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touro-synagogue">Touro Synagogue</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newport-rhode-island">Newport</a> opened in 1763. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rhode">Rhode</a> Islanders elected their own governors and assembly. Newport grew rich on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a>, including a large share of the colonial slave trade, while farms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narragansett-people">Narragansett</a> Country used enslaved labour.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rhode">Rhode</a> Islanders burned the British customs ship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspee-affair">Gaspee</a> in 1772, and on 4 May 1776 the colony became the first to renounce allegiance to the Crown. It joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, and its 1663 charter served as the state constitution until 1843.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/colony-of-rhode-island-and-providence-plantations.jpg" alt="Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: AnonMoos, based on image by Zscout370, AnonMoos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_ensign_of_Great_Britain_(1707%E2%80%931800,_square_canton).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1636 CE – 1776 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Newport</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Roger Williams, Benedict Arnold, Stephen Hopkins</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Baptists, Quakers and other Protestants</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rhode Island (colony)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1706&amp;focus=colony-of-rhode-island-and-providence-plantations" aria-label="Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2938 695 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Colony of Rhode Island and Providence Plantations lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3258 951L4970 663M3258 951L3277 927M3258 951L4997 651M3258 951L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" 